---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/translation-studies/foundational/what-translation-is">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Translation studies begins by exploding a common assumption: translation is not word
    substitution between two dictionaries. It is the transfer of <strong>meaning and
    function</strong> between languages — which makes it an act of analysis, judgement and
    writing in its own right, with its own theory, techniques and professional standards.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="meaning, analysis, equivalence, managed loss" widget="equationStep" config={equivCfg} />

  <h2>The spectrum: formal to dynamic</h2>
  <p>
    Nida's classic distinction still organises the craft. Formal equivalence keeps the source's
    structure visible (legal documents, scripture study); dynamic equivalence reproduces the
    reader's <em>experience</em> (marketing, literature). Most real texts live between the poles,
    and deciding where on the spectrum a given sentence belongs is the translator's judgement —
    guided by the text's function, not preference.
  </p>

  <h2>Loss is the honest baseline</h2>
  <p>
    Every translation loses something: wordplay, metre, cultural resonance, register. The
    professional skill is deliberate compensation — reproducing a pun's <em>effect</em> with a
    different pun, preserving formality where the joke was, footnoting where neither works.
    Unmanaged loss is the hallmark of machine-literal output; managed loss is the human value
    that post-editing still requires.
  </p>

  <h2>The profession's frame</h2>
  <p>
    Translation is also a trade with quality standards: the ATA certification exam scores against
    a published error typology (accuracy, terminology, grammar, style), and its ethics bind
    confidentiality and fidelity. Later units add the CAT tools, machine translation and
    specialisation domains — but all of it rests on this unit's move: translate the meaning,
    then check it against the reader.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> ATA Certification Program content outline; Nida &amp; Taber, <em>The Theory and Practice of Translation</em>; Munday, <em>Introducing Translation Studies</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
